  "notes": [
   "Parts are unchanged from step 1; none added or dropped.",
   "The catalogue's raspberry-pi-4 lists only a GPIO_HEADER port (no individual pins and no CSI port). I named the header pins GND and GPIO17 as the task instructions suggest, so the two wires can be told apart. The ribbon cable's Pi end has no port name because the catalogue has no CSI port.",
   "Wiring follows the circuit image. The black lead goes from a Pi GND pin to breadboard row 3 (hole 3t.a). The orange lead goes from GP17 to row 5 (hole 5t.a). The tutorial's Python code uses Button(17) and the Pi's internal pull-up, so no external resistor is used.",
   "The button straddles rows 3 and 5. Side 1 (1.l, 1.r) is in row 3, which is the GND row. Side 2 (2.l, 2.r) is in row 5, which is the GP17 row. Pressing the button connects GP17 to GND. The exact hole columns (c and e) are my reading of the image.",
   "In the breadboard catalogue naming, the picture's numbered rows are the 'column' number and the lettered a-e columns are the 'row' letter, so 3t.a is row 3, column a. The button sits in the top bank.",
   "The ribbon cable is the Pi 5 type from the lookup. The tutorial's cable is the standard Pi camera ribbon, and the cable is only a joiner."
  ]
 },
